          "reason": "unmet requirement: at least 22 non-empty lines, found 14",
          "text": "filename: docs/knife-sharpening.md\n---\n# How to Sharpen a Kitchen Knife by Hand\n\nSharpening a kitchen knife by hand is a simple yet essential skill that every cook should master. A well-sharpened knife not only performs better but also enhances safety in the kitchen. This guide will walk you through the process step by step, ensuring you achieve a sharp, durable edge.\n\n## Gather Your Tools\n\nBefore you begin, make sure you have all the necessary tools at hand. The most common tool for sharpening a knife by hand is a whetstone. A whetstone is a flat, abrasive stone that allows you to hone the edge of your knife with precision. Choose a whetstone that is appropriate for the type of knife you are sharpening—typically, a 1000-grit stone is suitable for general use.\n\nIn addition to the whetstone, you will need a stable surface to work on, such as a non-slip mat or a damp cloth. A small container of water or a lubricant is also useful, as it helps the stone maintain its effectiveness during the sharpening process. Lastly, a soft cloth or towel will be handy for drying the knife after sharpening.\n\n## Sharpen the Edge\n\nStart by holding the knife at a consistent angle, usually around 15 to 20 degrees, depending on the knife's design. This angle is crucial for maintaining the correct edge and ensuring the knife remains sharp for longer. The angle should be consistent throughout the sharpening process to avoid creating a weak or uneven edge.\n\nPlace the knife on the whetstone, with the edge just touching the surface. Begin by sliding the knife across the stone in a smooth, even motion, applying light pressure. Repeat this motion on both sides of the blade, ensuring that you maintain the same angle each time. This process will gradually remove metal from the edge, creating a sharp, honed point.\n\nContinue sharpening until you feel the edge begin to take shape. It is important to be patient and not rush the process. A well-sharpened knife will feel lighter and more responsive when cutting. If you are unsure about the angle or the process, consider practicing on a less critical knife first to build confidence.\n\n## Test and Store\n\nOnce you have sharpened the knife, it is time to test its performance. Use it to cut through a piece of paper or a tomato to see if it slices smoothly and cleanly. If the knife feels dull or struggles to cut, you may need to sharpen it further or adjust the angle.\n\nAfter testing, it is important to store the knife properly to maintain its sharpness. Keep it in a dry place, ideally in a knife block or a magnetic strip, to prevent it from coming into contact with other objects that could dull the edge. Regular maintenance, such as occasional honing with a whetstone, will help keep your knife in optimal condition for years to come.\n\nBy following these steps, you can ensure that your kitchen knife remains sharp, safe, and ready for use whenever you need it. With practice, you will become more proficient in the art of hand sharpening, making your time in the kitchen more efficient and enjoyable.",
